nd working-class Memphis voters, or movement in late polling, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Steve Cohen (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Steve Cohen's retirement leaves an open Memphis-area seat that was redrawn to be substantially more Republican, shifting from a Democratic stronghold to a district with a Republican-leaning partisan edge. State Senator Brent Taylor, backed by Trump, won the GOP primary and faces Justin Pearson, the high-profile 'Tennessee Three' state lawmaker.\n\nTaylor leads in fundraising from outside groups and holds a modest cash edge, though Pearson has raised more overall and drawn endorsements from Kamala Harris and National Nurses United. A September Hart poll put Taylor ahead 48-44, and prediction markets give Pearson only about a 13 percent chance. Most expert ratings call the seat a Republican flip, with a few rating it only Likely Republican given Pearson's profile and the district's Democratic voting history.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1065, "margin": -7.68}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.132, "margin": -8.0}]}